       (93836, 30194, 'nl', 'Harp Seal',
        'The Harp Seal is a species of earless seal, or true seal, native to the northernmost Atlantic Ocean and Arctic Ocean. They have a unique coloration that varies with age. Adult Harp Seals have a silvery-gray coat with a characteristic dark \\"harp\\" pattern on their back, while pups are born with a white, fluffy coat that helps them blend into the ice. Adult Harp Seals typically weigh between 300 to 400 pounds (136 to 181 kg) and measure about 5 to 6.5 feet (1.5 to 2 meters) in length.\\n\\nHarp Seals are carnivorous (piscivorous) animals, eating fish, including capelin, herring, cod, Arctic cod and halibut, and crabs and other invertebrates.\\n\\n'),
       (93837, 30194, 'fr', 'Harp Seal',
        'The Harp Seal is a species of earless seal, or true seal, native to the northernmost Atlantic Ocean and Arctic Ocean. They have a unique coloration that varies with age. Adult Harp Seals have a silvery-gray coat with a characteristic dark \\"harp\\" pattern on their back, while pups are born with a white, fluffy coat that helps them blend into the ice. Adult Harp Seals typically weigh between 300 to 400 pounds (136 to 181 kg) and measure about 5 to 6.5 feet (1.5 to 2 meters) in length.\\n\\nHarp Seals are carnivorous (piscivorous) animals, eating fish, including capelin, herring, cod, Arctic cod and halibut, and crabs and other invertebrates.\\n\\n'),
       (93838, 30194, 'es', 'Harp Seal',
        'The Harp Seal is a species of earless seal, or true seal, native to the northernmost Atlantic Ocean and Arctic Ocean. They have a unique coloration that varies with age. Adult Harp Seals have a silvery-gray coat with a characteristic dark \\"harp\\" pattern on their back, while pups are born with a white, fluffy coat that helps them blend into the ice. Adult Harp Seals typically weigh between 300 to 400 pounds (136 to 181 kg) and measure about 5 to 6.5 feet (1.5 to 2 meters) in length.\\n\\nHarp Seals are carnivorous (piscivorous) animals, eating fish, including capelin, herring, cod, Arctic cod and halibut, and crabs and other invertebrates.\\n\\n'),
       (93839, 30195, 'nl', 'Hawksbill Sea Turtle',
        'The Hawksbill Sea Turtle is a critically endangered sea turtle. It\\\'s appearance is similar to that of other marine turtles. In general, it has a flattened body shape, a protective carapace, and flipper-like limbs, adapted for swimming in the open ocean. It is easily distinguished from other sea turtles by its sharp, curving beak with prominent tomium, and the saw-like appearance of its shell margins. Hawksbill shells slightly change colors, depending on water temperature.\\n\\nHawksbill Sea Turtles are omnivores and feed mainly on sponges. They will also eat sea invertebrates, such as sea jellies, mollusks, fish, crustaceans, marine algae, and other sea animals and plants. \\n\\n'),
