       (93901, 30215, 'es', 'Marsh Deer',
        'The Marsh Deer, also known as Pampas Deer, is South America\\\'s largest deer species, weighing up to 150 kg (331 lbs). It inhabits wetlands and marshes, with long legs adapted for wading and swimming. Marsh Deer has reddish-brown fur with a white underbelly; short, dense hair for camouflage in wetlands.\\n\\nMarsh Deer are herbivorous, they feed on aquatic plants, grasses, and reeds. \\n\\n'),
       (93902, 30216, 'nl', 'Masked Puffer',
        'The Masked Pufferfish, also known as the Panda Puffer, is a pufferfish in the family Tetraodontidae. It has black patches around both eyes, lacks pelvic fins, and has the ability to inflate its body to almost twice its normal size. Additionally, when threatened or alarmed, it holds its spines straight out from the body. Not to be overlooked, it also has a fused beak-like structure that serves as its teeth. The puffer is among the most poisonous animals in the world, ranking second, after poison dart frogs.\\n\\nThe Masked Puffer is an omnivore, but has a diet mainly consisting of various invertebrates such as mollusks, sponges, algae, echinoderms, and crustaceans. It has also been known to consume coral from time to time.\\n\\n'),
       (93903, 30216, 'fr', 'Masked Puffer',
        'The Masked Pufferfish, also known as the Panda Puffer, is a pufferfish in the family Tetraodontidae. It has black patches around both eyes, lacks pelvic fins, and has the ability to inflate its body to almost twice its normal size. Additionally, when threatened or alarmed, it holds its spines straight out from the body. Not to be overlooked, it also has a fused beak-like structure that serves as its teeth. The puffer is among the most poisonous animals in the world, ranking second, after poison dart frogs.\\n\\nThe Masked Puffer is an omnivore, but has a diet mainly consisting of various invertebrates such as mollusks, sponges, algae, echinoderms, and crustaceans. It has also been known to consume coral from time to time.\\n\\n'),
