       (93992, 30246, 'nl', 'Short-Snouted Seahorse',
        'The Short-Snouted Seahorse, scientific name Hippocampus Hippocampus, is a species of seahorse within the family Syngnathidae. They have potential to be up to 15 centimetres (5.9 in) long. Seahorse bodies might be black, purple, orange, brown, green or yellow coloration permitting them to cover among the many vegetation. The Short-Snouted Seahorses have a tail that’s unable to bend backward however is taken into account semi-flexible. They use it as an anchor by wrapping it around coral or seagrass.\\n\\nShort-Snouted Seahorses are known to be ambush predators whom feed on live, moving food. On average the adult seahorse will eat roughly between 30 and 50 tiny shrimp a day. They will remain still until a small animal passes within reach and then grab it.\\n\\n'),
       (93993, 30246, 'fr', 'Short-Snouted Seahorse',
        'The Short-Snouted Seahorse, scientific name Hippocampus Hippocampus, is a species of seahorse within the family Syngnathidae. They have potential to be up to 15 centimetres (5.9 in) long. Seahorse bodies might be black, purple, orange, brown, green or yellow coloration permitting them to cover among the many vegetation. The Short-Snouted Seahorses have a tail that’s unable to bend backward however is taken into account semi-flexible. They use it as an anchor by wrapping it around coral or seagrass.\\n\\nShort-Snouted Seahorses are known to be ambush predators whom feed on live, moving food. On average the adult seahorse will eat roughly between 30 and 50 tiny shrimp a day. They will remain still until a small animal passes within reach and then grab it.\\n\\n'),
