       (94007, 30252, 'nl', 'South China Tiger',
        'The South China Tiger is a critically endangered subspecies. Once found in the forests of southern China, it is now considered functionally extinct in the wild, with no confirmed sightings for over 25 years. The South China tiger has a distinctive coat with broad, closely spaced black stripes on a yellowish-orange background. It is smaller than other tiger subspecies, with males weighing between 100 kg and 195 kg (221 lb - 430 lb) and measuring about 2.3 m to 2.8 m (7.5 ft - 9 ft) in length.\\n\\nAs carnivorous predators, South China tigers primarily hunt large ungulates such as deer and wild boar.\\n\\n'),
       (94008, 30252, 'fr', 'South China Tiger',
